Things to know about the new rows:

The three sub-day rows need the value field to accept fractions, and most of their CUIs are the bare amounts I flagged for review (“120 minutes”, “30M post”), which may be durations.
≤ 18 months has no CUI in your JSON. I added it because it is a common clinical window, but it will never match until a CUI is sourced for it.
≤ 12 weeks, ≤ 4 months and ≤ 20 years have only range wording (“6-12 Weeks”, “1-4 months”), with no plain “past 12 weeks” CUI.
Gaps that a days row cannot fix

These need a rule or a new field, so I did not add them as rows:

Most recent and All still have no CUI. Nothing in the JSON means “latest”, “current”, “ever” or “lifetime”. I tried to confirm UMLS CUIs for these by web search and could not, so someone with UMLS access needs to source them.
“More than X ago” (35 CUIs) still goes to All. To keep these, the lookup needs an “older than” flag alongside the row.
“Since an event” (last visit, admission, surgery, this pregnancy) still goes to All, the same as “from diagnosis”.
“Last N results” for comparisons, such as the latest CT plus the prior one, is a count and has no row.

The 1,215 excluded CUIs (lab timing, dosing frequency, age bands, billing) are unchanged in cui_to_bucket.csv and should still return no match.
